{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T202233Z-8ce7201b", "ts": "2026-11-26T20:26:43Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.1-dev", "branch": "chore/testament-cache-dogfood", "build": "f9e60c885bed"}, "body": {"base_commit": "0dc14b934c3920818b77537e338b472b06f10c51", "base_tree": "3059290a80d987552c4c176ebabb40c679b0409c", "head_tree": "d889800668589a5f10b393269acaa90f4e96edde", "head_commit": "4465bc8adf148eab2b036c8df36fc16f17f4c99d", "dispatch_commit": "4465bc8adf148eab2b036c8df36fc16f17f4c99d", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/entropy.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "tests/_hook_inproc.py", "tests/conftest.py", "tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py", "tests/test_entropy.py", "tests/test_hook_inproc.py", "tests/test_plugin_runtime.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/entropy.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "tests/_hook_inproc.py", "tests/conftest.py", "tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py", "tests/test_entropy.py", "tests/test_hook_inproc.py", "tests/test_plugin_runtime.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The suites that import run_plugin_hook still say it runs the hook as a real subprocess, and it now runs in-process", "recommendation": "Class. It broke because the change rewrote run_plugin_hook's mechanism (tests/test_plugin_runtime.py) and checked the prose in its own file, but not in the modules that import it. Core rule: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited'. tests/test_critic_session_guard.py:14 says its behavioural layer is 'the real CLI via subprocess (the reviewer's actual attack path)', and :33 calls run_plugin_hook 'the proven plugin-runtime subprocess harness'. Both are now false. A maintainer who trusts them and adds a process-boundary assertion there (stdout through a real pipe, the OS-reported exit status, interpreter writes for the write-isolation snapshot) gets an in-process call that cannot show it, and the test passes without testing anything. That is exactly the distinction the change drew when it moved four tests to run_plugin_hook_child. tests/test_session_boundary_events.py:63 ('loaded in-process for the one arm a subprocess cannot stage') implies that the file's other arms run as a subprocess, which is also no longer true. To fix it, grep every importer of run_plugin_hook (test_handoff_affordance, test_critic_session_guard, test_session_boundary_events, test_handoff_channel, test_stop_verdict_defer) for prose saying subprocess, child or real CLI. Delete each such claim or make it relational, for example 'through test_plugin_runtime.run_plugin_hook, which says how it runs'. If a test there really needs the process boundary, move it to run_plugin_hook_child.", "files": ["tests/test_plugin_runtime.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A prose claim, in a module that imports test_plugin_runtime.run_plugin_hook, that the hook runs there as a subprocess, child or real CLI.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "tests/test_critic_session_guard.py:14 (module docstring, 'the real CLI via subprocess')"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "tests/test_critic_session_guard.py:33 (comment, 'proven plugin-runtime subprocess harness')"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "tests/test_session_boundary_events.py:63 (comment, 'the one arm a subprocess cannot stage')"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The owner's ruling asks for the fix and a measurement of the gain; this change has no measurement and does not say it was deferred", "recommendation": "The authorizing ruling (owner-rulings.md, '2026-11-26 (test cacheability)' item 2, currently only on docs/test-cacheability-patterns / PR #1264) reads: 'This repo fixes its own two largest remaining blockers (evidence-id randomness; the subprocess hook runner) and measures the gain, as a separate change.' The change record and commit 9e0e452b both describe the fix, but neither records a before/after (for example the testament cacheability ranking or the cacheable-test count at base 0dc14b93 and at this head), and neither says the measurement is coming later. The consequence: the owner approved this work in order to get a gain, the change record's premise that these were 'two of the causes testament cacheability ranked highest' is never re-checked, and whoever orders #1218's remaining blockers next has no number to work from. Either record the measurement in the change record, with the command that re-derives it rather than only the digits, or state that it is deferred and put the deferral to the owner. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The randomness inventory exempts a seeded random.Random(...) as deterministic, but testament counts any random.Random draw, seeded or not, as entropy", "recommendation": "tests/test_entropy.py `_entropy_reads` skips `random.Random(<args>)` (\"seeded: deterministic\"), the same way determinism_lint does. testament's own cause words (`cause_words.PYTEST.kinds['entropy']`) say \"any random.Random draw, seeded or not\". So a seeded Random added to the plugin would pass `test_every_direct_randomness_read_is_accounted_for` and still make every test that reaches it uncacheable. No member exists today: the plugin has no `import random`. Relatedly, the `no_urandom` fixture only sees the Python-level `os.urandom` attribute. That is enough for uuid4 and the owner's own draw, but it cannot see C-level seeding. Nothing breaks today. If the inventory is meant to answer testament's question, it should count a seeded Random too.", "files": ["tests/test_entropy.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "mint_review_id now seeds its token but still reads the wall clock directly, so review-id-minting tests stay uncacheable on the clock axis", "recommendation": "The changed line in plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py `mint_review_id` routes the token through lib.entropy, but its stamp is still `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`, not `lib.clock`. The same holds at other sites in the module (lines ~1983, 2092, 2439). This looks deliberate, because `dispatch_age_minutes` compares the stamp with real time. The change record claims only the entropy cause, so nothing it says is false. A test that mints a review id still reads the clock, though, and testament still refuses to reuse its result. The clock reads could be left as they are, with that stated, or routed through lib.clock if those tests are meant to become cacheable.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no governed_by disposition to verify", "recommendation": "No plan claims this branch (scope unbound), and the change record (.prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md, oracle) carries no `governed_by:` field. So there is no recorded disposition whose truth against the diff could be checked. record_lint reports governed-by-gap 0. No action is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two hand-kept copies of one fake, held together by an uncacheable parity test, where one definition could produce both", "recommendation": "_write_mock_git writes a bash script, and _mock_stubs restates the same branch table in Python. TestMockGitParity, which must start real children, keeps the two equal. The parity test does catch drift, so nothing goes wrong silently, but every new probe (the symbolic-ref branch is one recent example) must be written twice. A simpler shape is a single Python answer table: either the shell script is generated from it, or the script is a python3 shim that calls the same function. That makes the parity class unnecessary. Optional, not required for this merge.", "files": ["tests/test_plugin_runtime.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Three more shell mock-git copies still start the hook as a child, outside the new stub mechanism", "recommendation": "tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py, tests/test_stop_abandoned_critic.py and tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py each have their own near-identical bash _write_mock_git, and each runs the hook as a python3 child, so their results stay uncacheable for the reason this change removed from run_plugin_hook. This is outside the change record's stated scope, which names only run_plugin_hook. If more cacheability work is planned, converge these on one answer table plus run_hook(stubs=...) rather than adding a Python twin per copy.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py", "tests/test_stop_abandoned_critic.py", "tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The test_critic_gate_fallthrough docstring now narrates the history of another file's harness", "recommendation": "The clause \"(as `test_plugin_runtime.py::TestPluginStopGate`'s was before it moved in-process with the mock answered there)\" describes how the code used to be. Delete it; the sentence is true without it. Nothing reads it, so no further edit is wanted.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The change record cites its authorizing ruling by branch name, and that branch's PR (#1264) is still open", "recommendation": "'recorded in owner-rulings.md on docs/test-cacheability-patterns' is accurate today: HEAD's and develop's owner-rulings.md do not yet carry the 2026-11-26 (test cacheability) entry. If this merges first, develop holds a change whose authority sits only on another open branch. Once #1264 merges and its branch is deleted, the branch name no longer resolves, though the dated heading still does. Citing the entry by its dated heading ('2026-11-26 (test cacheability)', item 2) holds in both cases. No bar clears: nobody acts wrongly on this citation.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/testament-cache-dogfood.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The fallthrough test's docstring now narrates where a sibling harness used to be", "recommendation": "tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py now says its harness is a subprocess 'as test_plugin_runtime.py::TestPluginStopGate's was before it moved in-process'. That is history, and it goes stale the next time the sibling changes. Stating only what this file does (a real child with a mock git on PATH, because the gate composes inside cmd_stop) would be enough. It is wording only, and nothing reads it.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Nothing reports when the test-only seed is active outside the suite", "recommendation": "If PRAWDUCT_TEST_ONLY_ENTROPY_SEED is well-formed in a real session's environment, ids become a pid-and-count sequence and nothing logs it. Within the same second the ids could then collide across processes that reuse a pid. Setting a variable whose name says TEST_ONLY takes deliberate misuse, so the good-faith cap makes this an observation. Recorded so the choice not to warn is visible.", "files": ["plugin/lib/entropy.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered set